      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To establish a method for the determination of free digoxin in serum for clinical use and to study the relationship between the free and total digoxin concentrations in chronic cardiac insufficiency patients receiving digoxin with different renal function."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The ultrafiltration with fluorescence polarization immunoassay was used to determine the concentration of free digoxin."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The concentrations of digoxin standards in serum were 0.96, 1.92, and 3.84 nmol.L-1. The relative standard deviation was < 7% for intra-day and < 6% for inter-day determinations. The average recovery was 99.95 +/- 2.18%. The ratio of free/total digoxin in chronic cardiac insufficiency patients with renal dysfunction was lower than that in patients with normal kidneys (63.5 +/- 4.7% vs 75.1 +/- 3.9%, P < 0.01)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The present method is simple and reliable. In these patients there is an over-measurement for total digoxin concentration, suggesting the presence of elevated endogenous digoxin-like immunoreactive substances."
        }
      ]},
